{% block title %}Internals · Cuprum{% endblock %}
{% block description %}How Cuprum keeps process boundaries legible: catalogue and builders construct, policy authorizes, the runtime executes, and observers report. The lifecycle, pipelines, and invariants.{% endblock %}
{% block main_class %} class="cu-internals"{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
{% set src = repository_url ~ '/blob/' ~ cuprum_tag ~ '/' %}
{% set sections = [
  {'id': 'division', 'label': 'Division of labour', 'title': 'Four jobs, four owners',
   'lede': 'Catalogue and builders construct. Policy authorizes. The runtime executes. Observers report. No component does another’s job.'},
  {'id': 'lifecycle', 'label': 'Execution lifecycle', 'title': 'One command, start to finish',
   'lede': 'What happens, in order, between calling a builder and holding a CommandResult — and what happens when the run is cut short.'},
  {'id': 'pipelines', 'label': 'Pipelines and streams', 'title': 'Pipes, pumps, and fail-fast',
   'lede': 'How stages are joined, where bytes are copied, how backpressure works, and which stage is blamed when one fails.'},
  {'id': 'policy', 'label': 'Policy and observation', 'title': 'Scopes, hooks, and sinks',
   'lede': 'Where the allowlist lives, how it narrows, and the channels that report on a run without changing what it does.'},
  {'id': 'sources', 'label': 'Sources', 'title': 'Read the plans'},
] %}
