      {{ ui.routemap(sections, 'Drafting room') }}

      {% call ui.section(sections, 'division') %}
      {{ ui.flow(architecture) }}
      <div class="cu-internals__invariants mt-14">
        <div>
          <p class="cu-kicker">Stated invariants</p>
          <h3 class="cu-internals__subhead">What holds, every time</h3>
          <p class="mt-3">
            Each line names where it is enforced. If the code and this list ever
            disagree, the code is right and this page is out of date.
          </p>
          {{ marks.town_hall('cu-internals__town-hall') }}
        </div>
        <ol class="cu-invariants">
          <li><strong>Nothing reaches a shell.</strong> Every spawn passes an argv tuple with <code>shell=False</code>. <span class="cu-invariants__src">cuprum/_wait4_process.py, cuprum/_pipeline_spawn.py</span></li>
          <li><strong>A command exists only for a catalogued program.</strong> <code>sh.make</code> resolves an explicit catalogue, else the scope’s, else the default, and raises <code>UnknownProgramError</code> when it does not list the program. <span class="cu-invariants__src">cuprum/sh/factory.py, cuprum/catalogue.py</span></li>
          <li><strong>Authorization happens at run time, before any spawn.</strong> The scope in force is checked for a command, and for every stage of a pipeline before the first stage starts. <span class="cu-invariants__src">cuprum/context/, cuprum/_pipeline_internals.py</span></li>
          <li><strong>Scopes only narrow.</strong> A nested <code>scoped()</code> intersects its allowlist with its parent’s. Widening is a separate, explicit call to <code>allow()</code>. <span class="cu-invariants__src">cuprum/context/</span></li>
          <li><strong>A binding never widens the allowlist.</strong> <code>bind_executable</code> decides only what a permitted program executes; resolution happens after the allowlist check, and a refused program never calls its resolver. <span class="cu-invariants__src">cuprum/context/, cuprum/executable_binding.py</span></li>
          <li><strong>Every spawned child is reaped.</strong> Cancellation, timeouts, and <code>terminate()</code> on a handle escalate from <code>SIGTERM</code> to <code>SIGKILL</code> and wait for the exit, even under repeated cancellation. Only a run that opts into <code>OWN_GROUP</code> signals a process group, and only the group its spawn created. <span class="cu-invariants__src">cuprum/_process_lifecycle.py, cuprum/_group_teardown.py</span></li>
          <li><strong>Capture is complete.</strong> Truncating an echoed line, or disabling echo for a sink that cannot encode the text, never removes bytes from the result. <span class="cu-invariants__src">cuprum/_stream_echo.py</span></li>
          <li><strong>Python is the behavioural reference.</strong> The optional Rust pump must produce identical bytes, and a hop it cannot take runs on the Python pump instead. <span class="cu-invariants__src">cuprum/_pipeline_streams.py</span></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      {% endcall %}

      {% call ui.section(sections, 'lifecycle', class='cu-section--tint') %}
      {{ ui.flow(lifecycle) }}
      {% endcall %}

      {% call ui.section(sections, 'pipelines') %}
      {{ ui.flow(pipeline) }}
      <div class="cu-columns mt-12">
        <div class="cu-prose">
          <h3>Backpressure</h3>
          <p>
            Stages are joined by operating-system pipes. Between two stages a
            pump reads from one and writes to the next, awaiting
            <code>drain()</code> on each write, so a slow consumer slows its
            producer instead of filling memory.
          </p>
          <h3>Where Rust fits</h3>
          <p>
            That inter-stage pump is the one place the optional
            <a href="/cuprum/internals/rust-extension/">Rust extension</a> is used: when
            it is installed and selected, the copy runs outside the GIL, using
            <code>splice()</code> on Linux. Capturing the final output always
            uses the Python path.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="cu-prose">
          <h3>Diagnosing a fail-fast</h3>
          <p>
            When the first non-final failure still has stages to stop, observe
            hooks receive one <code>pipeline_fail_fast</code> event before
            termination begins, carrying the stage index, pipeline width, exit
            code, and duration. It omits argv and tags, so command-line secrets
            do not reach the warning it produces.
          </p>
          <p>
            A final-stage failure, or one whose siblings have already exited,
            emits no such event: there was nothing left to terminate. It is
            still reported as <code>failure_index</code>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      {% endcall %}

      {% call ui.section(sections, 'policy', class='cu-section--tint') %}
      <div class="cu-columns">
        <div class="cu-prose">
          <h3>Where the policy lives</h3>
          <p>
            <code>CuprumContext</code> is an immutable value held in a
            <code>ContextVar</code>. Each thread gets its own; each async task
            inherits its creator’s and may change it independently. Entering
            <code>scoped()</code> replaces the value, and leaving the block
            restores the previous one, even on an exception.
          </p>
          <p>
            At the root, with no scope established, every catalogued program
            may run. That permissive default eases adoption; narrowing it is
            how code states its policy. An explicitly empty allowlist inside a
            restricted scope permits nothing. A scope made from a catalogue
            also makes that catalogue the one <code>sh.make</code> consults,
            while a scope that only narrows the allowlist leaves it alone.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two more policies ride on the same context. <code>EnvMode</code>
            decides whether a child inherits, overlays, or replaces the
            parent's environment, and an executable binding decides which
            file a permitted program runs, without changing the name the
            allowlist, metadata, and telemetry carry.
          </p>
          <h3>Hooks</h3>
          <p>
            Before hooks run in registration order, parent first. After hooks
            run in reverse, child first, so cleanup nests like context
            managers. <code>logging_hook()</code> pairs the two to log start
            and exit.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="cu-prose">
          <h3>Observation</h3>
          <p>
            <code>sh.observe()</code> delivers structured <code>ExecEvent</code>
            values — plan, start, stdout, stderr, exit, one settled event with
            the bounded terminal outcome, and the ancillary timeout, teardown,
            and group-teardown events — each correlated by an
            <code>exec_id</code> rather than a pid the system may recycle.
            Narrower facts travel on channels of their own: what a control
            operation did to a child, an echo that fell back, a line stream's
            lifecycle, and the Rust pump's routing decisions.
          </p>
          <p>
            The adapters in <code>cuprum.adapters</code> turn those events into
            structured logs, Prometheus-style metrics, and OpenTelemetry-style
            spans. They define protocols rather than importing a telemetry
            library, so Cuprum keeps its zero-dependency install.
          </p>
          {% call ui.callout('caveat', 'Arguments are logged verbatim') %}
          <p>
            The structured logging adapter and <code>logging_hook()</code>
            record argv as supplied. Pass secrets through the environment or a
            file, not on the command line.
          </p>
          {% endcall %}
          <h3>Presentation sinks</h3>
          <p>
            An opt-in sink reframes a run’s echoed output without changing
            capture or the result. <code>GitHubActionsSink</code> wraps a run in
            a collapsible log group and turns a failure into one annotation; it
            activates only when <code>GITHUB_ACTIONS</code> is
            <code>true</code>, unless forced.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      {% endcall %}

      {% call ui.section(sections, 'sources') %}
